WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00.300 --> 00:01.796
In this video, I want to give you

00:01.796 --> 00:03.730
some context for where we're going with this course.

00:04.290 --> 00:06.908
And in short, where we're going is

00:06.908 --> 00:09.900
getting structured output from an LLM using Pydantic.

00:10.368 --> 00:12.323
So, the simplest way you might think

00:12.323 --> 00:14.278
about getting structured output from an LLM

00:14.589 --> 00:16.710
is we'll just ask for it. And

00:16.960 --> 00:18.804
what I mean by that is that

00:18.804 --> 00:20.911
you can indicate in the prompt that you're

00:20.911 --> 00:23.020
passing to the LLM that you want the

00:23.020 --> 00:25.129
response to be formatted in a particular way.

00:25.640 --> 00:27.230
So, one really common approach

00:27.570 --> 00:29.765
is to ask for a response in what's called JavaScript

00:29.765 --> 00:32.795
object notation or JSON format.

00:33.496 --> 00:35.129
And if you're not already familiar

00:35.129 --> 00:37.036
with JSON format, no need to worry.

00:37.396 --> 00:39.930
You'll learn everything you need to know in this

00:39.930 --> 00:42.466
course about working with LLM responses in JSON format.

00:43.106 --> 00:45.036
So in this course, you're going to

00:45.036 --> 00:46.966
be building up a customer support system

00:46.966 --> 00:49.256
that we were looking at in the last video.

00:49.435 --> 00:50.525
And so to get a sense

00:50.525 --> 00:51.795
of how this is going to work,

00:51.795 --> 00:53.845
you can imagine you have a system where

00:53.845 --> 00:55.496
the user can fill out a form,

00:55.756 --> 00:58.731
indicating their name and email, and a request.

00:59.392 --> 01:01.642
So, in this case, you have Joe User

01:01.642 --> 01:03.981
here who ordered a remote control plane kit,

01:03.981 --> 01:05.906
but found that it arrived missing some

01:05.906 --> 01:07.831
parts. And now wants to return it.

01:08.281 --> 01:10.781
You can then construct a prompt that looks like this,

01:10.781 --> 01:14.391
where you're asking the LLM to analyze the user query.

01:14.391 --> 01:16.691
And then here in curly braces,

01:16.691 --> 01:19.231
you're passing the user input into the prompt.

01:19.231 --> 01:21.852
And then you ask for a response in JSON format

01:21.852 --> 01:23.772
that follows this example

01:23.772 --> 01:25.231
where you have all the fields you

01:25.231 --> 01:26.691
want to have filled out, like name

01:26.867 --> 01:29.668
email and query from the user input,

01:29.668 --> 01:31.668
and then a priority, category, whether or

01:31.668 --> 01:33.668
not it's a complaint and some tags.

01:33.668 --> 01:35.567
And so this is just a hypothetical set

01:35.567 --> 01:37.468
of things that you might be asking for.

01:37.468 --> 01:40.768
You could be asking for whatever JSON structure you want here.

01:40.768 --> 01:43.867
And then you pass that prompt to an LLM,

01:43.867 --> 01:46.219
and what you would be hoping for, in

01:46.219 --> 01:48.867
this case, is a response that looks like this,

01:48.867 --> 01:53.067
where the name, email, and query fields contain the user input.

01:53.067 --> 01:55.823
And then you have your priority, category, is_complaint,

01:55.823 --> 01:58.924
complaint and tags all filled out based on that user input.

01:59.263 --> 02:02.144
So, JSON structure is similar to a Python dictionary,

02:02.563 --> 02:04.924
where you open and close with these curly braces

02:05.243 --> 02:08.403
and then inside, you have this key value pair format.

02:08.804 --> 02:11.024
So in the prompt in this case, you've just said,

02:11.343 --> 02:12.883
here's the input from the user

02:13.024 --> 02:16.984
and I want you to provide a response in exactly this format,

02:17.183 --> 02:20.063
including the name, email, query and so on.

02:20.663 --> 02:22.663
And then with this structured output,

02:22.824 --> 02:25.941
you can automatically create a support ticket in your system.

02:25.941 --> 02:28.941
or decide whether to call a tool or what to do next.

02:29.580 --> 02:31.786
It turns out that when you ask an LLM

02:31.786 --> 02:33.991
to give you back a structured response like this,

02:33.991 --> 02:35.767
in many cases it can come

02:35.767 --> 02:37.841
pretty close. But it's not always perfect.

02:38.191 --> 02:41.700
For example, it might add additional text in the response, like

02:41.931 --> 02:43.911
here's the JSON output you requested.

02:44.131 --> 02:46.099
Or it might add some other formatting

02:46.099 --> 02:48.069
like this triple back tick markdown formatting

02:48.069 --> 02:51.961
is really common in LLM responses when you're asking for JSON.

02:52.289 --> 02:53.736
And beyond that, it might not

02:53.736 --> 02:55.556
give you all the fields you expect.

02:55.556 --> 02:57.116
or it could end up formatting the

02:57.116 --> 02:58.676
fields in a way that's not usable.

02:58.676 --> 03:01.637
Like maybe here getting the email format wrong.

03:01.637 --> 03:03.562
And it's this unpredictability of the response

03:03.562 --> 03:05.486
format that makes it hard to rely

03:05.486 --> 03:08.467
on LLMs to directly provide you with structured output.

03:08.467 --> 03:10.676
And this is where Pydantic comes in.

03:10.676 --> 03:13.681
With Pydantic, you define data models that specify

03:13.681 --> 03:16.687
the structure and types of data you expect.

03:16.687 --> 03:19.689
So in Python, this is what it would look like to define

03:19.689 --> 03:22.693
a pydantic data model for the request we were just looking at.

03:22.693 --> 03:26.552
where you have fields for name, email, query, and all the rest.

03:27.552 --> 03:29.502
What you're doing with a pydantic model like this

03:29.603 --> 03:32.562
is defining both the field names and the data types

03:32.562 --> 03:34.143
for each field in your model.

03:34.752 --> 03:38.513
So here, name, query, and priority are defined to be strings.

03:38.962 --> 03:40.600
The email field is defined as

03:40.600 --> 03:42.513
a special pydantic data type of EmailStr

03:43.062 --> 03:44.693
that's looking for a specific email formatting.

03:45.062 --> 03:47.562
And then the category field is defined as a Literal type

03:47.562 --> 03:49.932
that can only take on one of a few specific values.

03:49.932 --> 03:53.848
In this case, refund_request, request, information_request, or 'other'.

03:54.408 --> 03:57.778
And then is_complaint is a bool, that'll just be true or false.

03:58.188 --> 04:00.519
and tags is defined as a List[str].

04:01.848 --> 04:04.060
You can then use this pydantic data model

04:04.060 --> 04:06.549
to validate the response you receive from an LLM

04:06.899 --> 04:09.158
to ensure that it matches your expectations.

04:09.528 --> 04:12.371
In this course, you'll learn about two ways to

04:12.371 --> 04:15.528
get structured output from an LLM using pydantic data models.

04:15.818 --> 04:17.899
The first, and perhaps simplest way,

04:18.119 --> 04:20.605
is to just prompt the LLM to give you structured

04:20.605 --> 04:22.665
output, giving an example in the prompt

04:22.665 --> 04:24.725
of what kind of structure you want.

04:24.725 --> 04:26.838
And then take that LLM response,

04:26.838 --> 04:29.305
which you're hoping is in JSON format,

04:29.305 --> 04:31.834
and attempt to create an instance of your data model.

04:31.834 --> 04:35.485
In this case, your CustomerQuery data model that you've defined up here.

04:35.485 --> 04:38.185
using that LLM response as input.

04:38.185 --> 04:40.425
So, that's what this line of code

04:40.425 --> 04:42.985
is doing right here with this model_validate_json method.

04:42.985 --> 04:47.214
If the LLM response contains extra unexpected text or formatting,

04:47.214 --> 04:49.562
or if the JSON itself is not properly

04:49.562 --> 04:52.461
formatted, then this step will fail with a validation error

04:52.461 --> 04:56.362
letting you know that there was a problem with the JSON input.

04:56.711 --> 04:59.052
If on the other hand, the JSON format is valid,

04:59.331 --> 05:02.101
but the data contained in the JSON doesn't match your model,

05:02.372 --> 05:04.047
then this step will fail with

05:04.047 --> 05:06.002
a validation error letting you know that

05:06.002 --> 05:07.956
there's a mismatch between your model expectations

05:07.956 --> 05:09.911
and the JSON that you put in.

05:10.531 --> 05:13.661
So if this model validate JSON step is successful,

05:13.971 --> 05:16.882
and it's really kind of two steps happening behind the scenes.

05:17.081 --> 05:20.237
First parsing the JSON, and then using that to create

05:20.237 --> 05:21.918
an instance of your Pydantic data model.

05:21.918 --> 05:23.658
If all that runs without error,

05:23.658 --> 05:26.218
then you're working with validated data.

05:26.218 --> 05:27.879
and you're ready to pass that data

05:27.879 --> 05:29.778
on to the next component in your system.

05:30.418 --> 05:33.007
But if the validation of your LLM response fails,

05:33.007 --> 05:35.987
then what you can do is simply catch that validation error

05:37.108 --> 05:39.408
and pass it back to the LLM in a follow-up request,

05:39.408 --> 05:41.987
asking it to correct the problem that caused the error.

05:42.487 --> 05:43.903
Often this works pretty well. And

05:43.903 --> 05:45.555
you can even run through multiple error

05:45.555 --> 05:47.027
catching and correction cycles if you

05:47.027 --> 05:48.744
don't get a good result straight away.

05:49.043 --> 05:51.634
But it turns out that there's an even more reliable approach.

05:51.634 --> 05:54.004
You can now use with many of the

05:54.004 --> 05:56.374
current LLM APIs and agent frameworks out there.

05:56.744 --> 05:59.069
And that's to pass your Pydantic data model in

05:59.069 --> 06:01.394
as part of the initial request to the LLM.

06:01.843 --> 06:05.394
That way you're expressing exactly what you want in your API call.

06:05.394 --> 06:07.737
and you can more reliably get the data

06:07.737 --> 06:10.374
that you need in a format that you expect.

06:10.483 --> 06:13.269
In some cases, what's happening behind the scenes when you're

06:13.269 --> 06:16.053
passing your Pydantic model in the API call is that

06:16.600 --> 06:18.521
that this prompting and retry and validation

06:18.521 --> 06:20.720
logic is just being handled automatically for you.

06:21.329 --> 06:24.860
And in other cases, the LLM provider is using an approach known

06:24.860 --> 06:28.389
as constrained generation to make sure you get valid JSON every time.

06:28.819 --> 06:30.786
And you'll get a chance to play with

06:30.786 --> 06:33.000
frameworks using both of these approaches in the lessons.

06:33.220 --> 06:37.319
Another important use case for Pydantic and LLM workflows is tool calling.

06:38.029 --> 06:40.250
For example, with that user query we looked

06:40.250 --> 06:42.470
at before that says I forgot my password,

06:42.899 --> 06:45.339
you could pass that input to an LLM and have it

06:45.456 --> 06:48.136
provide a structured JSON response that looks like this.

06:48.776 --> 06:51.855
And then you might want to pass that JSON to another LLM

06:52.295 --> 06:54.276
and give it the option to call

06:54.276 --> 06:56.255
a tool based on the user's query.

06:56.615 --> 06:58.379
So in this case, you might want

06:58.379 --> 07:00.396
the LLM to call an FAQ lookup tool,

07:00.745 --> 07:02.536
which you can think of as just another

07:02.536 --> 07:04.326
Python function that you've defined in your code.

07:04.685 --> 07:06.456
that can return the appropriate response.

07:06.456 --> 07:08.218
Like, here maybe a password reset

07:08.218 --> 07:10.276
link and some instructions for the user.

07:10.495 --> 07:12.360
And so the way Pydantic comes into play

07:12.360 --> 07:14.225
for tool calling like this is that you

07:14.341 --> 07:16.081
So first define a pydantic data model

07:16.081 --> 07:19.742
that specifies the parameters for that function call.

07:20.201 --> 07:24.211
So here you're defining a pydantic model called FAQLookupArgs.

07:24.762 --> 07:26.721
And this model has two fields, one

07:26.721 --> 07:28.682
for the user query and the other

07:28.682 --> 07:30.477
for the tags associated with that

07:30.477 --> 07:32.572
user query. And this is just hypothetical.

07:32.891 --> 07:35.891
The types of things you might be passing in if

07:35.891 --> 07:39.192
you're trying to search for a particular frequently asked question response.

07:39.882 --> 07:43.041
And then you have your lookup_faq_answer function,

07:43.298 --> 07:46.778
that's defined to take those FAQLookupArgs as input.

07:47.059 --> 07:49.399
So, this is just a regular old Python function

07:49.399 --> 07:52.629
that looks up an FAQ answer by matching tags

07:52.629 --> 07:55.858
and keywords in a query to FAQ entry keywords.

07:56.358 --> 07:57.789
And I don't have the body of

07:57.789 --> 07:59.221
the function filled out here, but you'll

07:59.221 --> 08:00.652
see how all this works when we

08:00.652 --> 08:02.288
get into the code later in the lessons.

08:02.798 --> 08:04.670
And then you can define a tool

08:04.670 --> 08:06.809
you can use in your LLM API call.

08:07.249 --> 08:09.579
And the way that looks, at least for

08:09.579 --> 08:11.908
an API call to OpenAI in this case,

08:12.254 --> 08:14.475
is that you specify a type of function

08:14.475 --> 08:16.584
and then provide the name of the

08:16.584 --> 08:18.694
function, the description of what it does,

08:18.694 --> 08:21.095
and then the input parameters it takes.

08:21.095 --> 08:24.264
And this is where your Pydantic data model comes in.

08:24.264 --> 08:27.225
By passing in this model JSON schema

08:27.225 --> 08:29.035
from your Pydantic model,

08:29.035 --> 08:31.509
you're telling the LLM exactly what type

08:31.509 --> 08:33.985
of input parameters your function tool takes.

08:33.985 --> 08:35.443
And with that, you can make

08:35.443 --> 08:37.144
an API call that looks like this,

08:37.144 --> 08:39.495
where you're passing your tool definition

08:39.495 --> 08:42.610
in the tools parameter. And that tells the LLM

08:42.980 --> 08:44.455
that it has the option to use

08:44.455 --> 08:46.140
that tool if the messages in the prompt

08:46.140 --> 08:49.780
indicate that would be a good next step.

08:50.110 --> 08:52.110
If the LLM decides to call the tool,

08:52.471 --> 08:56.061
then it will return the parameters needed to call that function.

08:56.270 --> 09:00.091
You can then use your FAQLookupArgs pydantic model

09:00.091 --> 09:03.520
to validate that the parameters provided by the LLM

09:03.520 --> 09:05.311
are indeed what the function expects.

09:05.850 --> 09:08.041
And then you can call that function with those parameters

09:08.041 --> 09:10.167
and pass the result on to the next step in your

09:10.167 --> 09:11.855
system or back to the LLM

09:11.855 --> 09:13.826
to complete the rest of the response.

09:14.307 --> 09:16.631
So in this course, you'll learn these different

09:16.631 --> 09:18.956
methods for getting structured output from an LLM,

09:18.956 --> 09:21.116
in the form of a JSON response

09:21.116 --> 09:23.277
or the parameters to call a function.

09:23.576 --> 09:26.067
And you'll do this using pydantic data models.

09:26.647 --> 09:29.287
But before diving into validating LLM responses,

09:29.287 --> 09:31.376
to get started, we're going to take a

09:31.376 --> 09:33.466
look at the basics of pydantic models themselves.

09:33.986 --> 09:36.226
And to do that, we're going to zoom in

09:36.726 --> 09:39.006
first on the user input portion of this.

09:39.124 --> 09:40.884
customer support system you're going to build.

09:41.344 --> 09:42.874
So in the next lesson, you're going to start

09:43.302 --> 09:46.687
by looking at how you can take user input that's coming to

09:46.687 --> 09:48.379
you in the form of a

09:48.379 --> 09:50.354
Python dictionary or as a JSON string.

09:50.354 --> 09:53.489
and what you'll do is define a Pydantic data model to

09:53.489 --> 09:56.624
validate that your user input is in the format you expect,

09:56.884 --> 09:59.124
and that it contains the data you expect.

09:59.453 --> 10:01.033
So to start with, you'll just have

10:01.374 --> 10:04.083
a name and query fields defined as strings

10:04.423 --> 10:06.754
and an email field defined as an EmailStr.

10:07.234 --> 10:09.700
And then you'll use that Python dictionary or JSON string

10:09.700 --> 10:12.240
that contains the user input to create an instance

10:12.240 --> 10:14.679
of your user input data model.

10:15.120 --> 10:17.000
And with this, you'll get a sense of how

10:17.000 --> 10:19.629
pydantic data models work and how you can use them

10:19.629 --> 10:22.259
to validate, in this case, user input data.

10:22.850 --> 10:25.149
As I said before, where we're ultimately going

10:25.149 --> 10:27.879
with this course is using pydantic

10:27.879 --> 10:30.539
to get validated structured output from an LLM.

10:31.029 --> 10:32.165
But before we get to that,

10:32.165 --> 10:33.490
I'll see you in the next lesson

10:33.490 --> 10:36.710
to look at the basics of pydantic data models.